WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:05.725
Welcome to Module 2 of the Privileged Access
learning path: emergency access first.

2
00:00:05.725 --> 00:00:12.048
This module serves the project plan's
task A0.4 in Phase 0 and A1.1 in Phase 1,

3
00:00:12.048 --> 00:00:15.737
with Chapter 02 of the developer workbook.

4
00:00:15.737 --> 00:00:18.475
It's the one step in the
project that can't move.

5
00:00:18.475 --> 00:00:24.356
In it you check each emergency access
account against Microsoft Learn's standard,

6
00:00:24.356 --> 00:00:27.037
from code, with rules
E A oh one to E A ten.

7
00:00:27.037 --> 00:00:30.383
You build the alert on their
use as a reviewed template

8
00:00:30.383 --> 00:00:33.876
that also sees non-interactive
sign-ins and audit events.

9
00:00:33.876 --> 00:00:35.387
And you keep a drill record.

10
00:00:35.387 --> 00:00:40.188
The code runs on your laptop against
the same fictional tenant as Module 1.

11
00:00:40.188 --> 00:00:45.660
It's illustrative: tested against fixtures,
not run against a production tenant.

12
00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:50.422
Why first?

13
00:00:50.422 --> 00:00:54.627
The project plan's critical path says
that switching on approval before

14
00:00:54.627 --> 00:00:58.972
the emergency accounts exist is the
one sequence that locks the agency out.

15
00:00:58.972 --> 00:01:01.411
And Microsoft Learn documents the case.

16
00:01:01.411 --> 00:01:06.195
Every Global Administrator and Privileged
Role Administrator is eligible,

17
00:01:06.195 --> 00:01:09.410
approval is required, and
no approvers are selected.

18
00:01:09.410 --> 00:01:12.598
So no one can approve, and
administration is locked.

19
00:01:12.598 --> 00:01:15.935
That's why every later module
that writes leans on this one.

20
00:01:15.935 --> 00:01:20.735
Module 3 keeps the emergency group
excluded from every policy file.

21
00:01:20.735 --> 00:01:25.054
Module 4 refuses any PIM change
until the emergency accounts

22
00:01:25.054 --> 00:01:29.035
hold Global Administrator,
active, permanent and direct.

23
00:01:29.035 --> 00:01:32.510
And Module 7 refuses a review
that would remove them.

24
00:01:32.510 --> 00:01:34.911
The emergency accounts are also the main

25
00:01:34.911 --> 00:01:37.948
exception to zero permanently
active assignments.

26
00:01:37.948 --> 00:01:43.484
Module 1 reported E A oh one as Info only
because the exceptions list names it.

27
00:01:43.484 --> 00:01:46.800
Here you make sure the exception
is what it claims to be.

28
00:01:50.550 --> 00:01:53.390
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module,

29
00:01:53.390 --> 00:01:56.037
which takes about fifty-five
minutes with the lab.

30
00:01:56.037 --> 00:01:58.288
You'll explain the documented lockout,

31
00:01:58.288 --> 00:02:02.437
and why the emergency accounts come
before any approval is switched on.

32
00:02:02.437 --> 00:02:06.589
You'll name what Microsoft Learn's
standard asks of each emergency account,

33
00:02:06.589 --> 00:02:08.463
and the rule that checks each item.

34
00:02:08.463 --> 00:02:12.033
You'll explain why the
alert reads three tables,

35
00:02:12.033 --> 00:02:15.512
not one, and why it matches
object I D's, not names.

36
00:02:15.512 --> 00:02:17.962
You'll run the check
and read its findings.

37
00:02:17.962 --> 00:02:21.776
And you'll say why every enforced
Conditional Access policy

38
00:02:21.776 --> 00:02:25.437
must exclude the accounts, and
why a report-only one needn't.

39
00:02:25.437 --> 00:02:27.713
Before you start, finish Module 1:

40
00:02:27.713 --> 00:02:31.588
this lab uses its module and
its fictional tenant again.

41
00:02:31.588 --> 00:02:35.162
Reading K Q L helps for the
alert, but isn't required.

42
00:02:35.162 --> 00:02:38.064
Lab 2 needs a worldwide
or G C C test tenant

43
00:02:38.064 --> 00:02:41.920
with a test Azure
subscription, never production.

44
00:02:45.670 --> 00:02:48.083
Unit 1: the standard and the check.

45
00:02:48.083 --> 00:02:53.032
Microsoft Learn's page on managing
emergency access accounts sets the standard.

46
00:02:53.032 --> 00:02:58.420
Two or more accounts, cloud-only, on the
tenant's on-microsoft dot com domain.

47
00:02:58.420 --> 00:03:01.145
Passkey or certificate-based
authentication.

48
00:03:01.145 --> 00:03:04.695
Global Administrator, active
and permanent in PIM.

49
00:03:04.695 --> 00:03:06.467
Excluded, through one group,

50
00:03:06.467 --> 00:03:10.857
from the Conditional Access policies
that block or restrict sign-in.

51
00:03:10.857 --> 00:03:13.546
And validated at least every ninety days.

52
00:03:13.546 --> 00:03:14.895
Each item has a rule.

53
00:03:14.895 --> 00:03:17.020
E A oh one counts the accounts.

54
00:03:17.020 --> 00:03:18.510
Oh two checks the domain,

55
00:03:18.510 --> 00:03:22.533
and oh three that the account isn't
synchronized from on-premises.

56
00:03:22.533 --> 00:03:26.608
Oh five wants Global Administrator
active, permanent and direct.

57
00:03:26.608 --> 00:03:31.052
Oh six flags any method that
isn't phishing-resistant: phone,

58
00:03:31.052 --> 00:03:35.769
Authenticator, software OATH,
email or a Temporary Access Pass.

59
00:03:35.769 --> 00:03:40.382
Oh seven and oh eight check the exclusion
group, and oh nine the drill record.

60
00:03:40.382 --> 00:03:45.552
The program adds dual custody and a
post-use review, and two rules of its

61
00:03:45.552 --> 00:03:51.080
own design: oh four, a disabled account,
and E A ten, a stranger in the group.

62
00:03:54.830 --> 00:03:57.055
Pay attention to E A oh eight.

63
00:03:57.055 --> 00:04:00.485
Microsoft Learn says to
exclude the emergency accounts

64
00:04:00.485 --> 00:04:03.405
from the policies that
block or restrict sign-in.

65
00:04:03.405 --> 00:04:06.555
The code doesn't try to
judge which policies do that.

66
00:04:06.555 --> 00:04:12.030
It checks every enforced policy, and that
choice is this workbook's design guidance.

67
00:04:12.030 --> 00:04:15.966
An enforced policy that doesn't
exclude an emergency account,

68
00:04:15.966 --> 00:04:18.668
through the group or
directly, is a finding.

69
00:04:18.668 --> 00:04:23.067
A report-only policy that doesn't
exclude the group is only Info.

70
00:04:23.067 --> 00:04:27.246
Microsoft Learn says report-only
policies need no exclusion,

71
00:04:27.246 --> 00:04:31.343
and a report-only policy is how
you record emergency sign-ins.

72
00:04:31.343 --> 00:04:35.292
But the exclusion is required
before the policy is enforced.

73
00:04:35.292 --> 00:04:40.315
And the program's own pre-merge check,
which Module 3 builds, is stricter:

74
00:04:40.315 --> 00:04:44.818
it wants the exclusion on every
policy file, whatever its state.

75
00:04:44.818 --> 00:04:47.300
Module 3 comes back to this.

76
00:04:51.050 --> 00:04:52.725
Unit 2: the alert.

77
00:04:52.725 --> 00:04:55.840
Microsoft Learn's page
has three sample queries,

78
00:04:55.840 --> 00:04:59.500
and all three read the sign-in
log of interactive sign-ins.

79
00:04:59.500 --> 00:05:01.750
The example adds two more tables.

80
00:05:01.750 --> 00:05:03.718
The non-interactive sign-in log,

81
00:05:03.718 --> 00:05:08.334
because a refreshed token in a stolen
session is a non-interactive sign-in,

82
00:05:08.334 --> 00:05:12.042
and Microsoft Learn says new
sign-ins that obtain a refresh

83
00:05:12.042 --> 00:05:15.675
token with FIDO2 keys are
logged as non-interactive too.

84
00:05:15.675 --> 00:05:19.587
And the audit log, for a role change
made by an emergency account,

85
00:05:19.587 --> 00:05:24.025
and a security method registered on
one, which is the start of a takeover.

86
00:05:24.025 --> 00:05:26.488
The query matches object I D's only,

87
00:05:26.488 --> 00:05:31.238
so a rename can't hide a sign-in,
and it refuses a user principal name.

88
00:05:31.238 --> 00:05:36.243
Then the kit wraps it in an A R M
template, with Microsoft Learn's severity

89
00:05:36.243 --> 00:05:41.249
zero and a threshold of greater than
zero, evaluated every five minutes.

90
00:05:41.249 --> 00:05:42.550
It deploys nothing.

91
00:05:42.550 --> 00:05:45.680
Deployment is the reviewed pipeline's job.

92
00:05:49.430 --> 00:05:50.155
Try it.

93
00:05:50.155 --> 00:05:53.128
Lab 1 runs in the
privileged-access folder,

94
00:05:53.128 --> 00:05:57.205
in one PowerShell 7 session,
against the fictional tenant.

95
00:05:57.205 --> 00:06:02.480
Step one runs Microsoft Learn's queries
over a sign-in export, with Pester.

96
00:06:02.480 --> 00:06:04.418
Seven pass and none fail.

97
00:06:04.418 --> 00:06:09.018
The tests show what they find: of
five emergency events in the export,

98
00:06:09.018 --> 00:06:14.030
Learn's two-I D query finds the two
interactive sign-ins, and misses the rest.

99
00:06:14.030 --> 00:06:18.130
Step two loads the module and lists
the permissions the check needs.

100
00:06:18.130 --> 00:06:22.782
There are six: Group Member dot Read
Basic dot All, Policy dot Read dot All,

101
00:06:22.782 --> 00:06:26.330
Role Assignment Schedule to
read, User dot Read dot All,

102
00:06:26.330 --> 00:06:31.218
User dot Read Basic dot All, and User
Authentication Method dot Read dot All.

103
00:06:31.218 --> 00:06:32.304
Nothing writes.

104
00:06:32.304 --> 00:06:35.330
The output on the slide is wrapped to fit.

105
00:06:39.080 --> 00:06:43.555
Step three checks the fictional tenant's
accounts and writes the alert template.

106
00:06:43.555 --> 00:06:48.014
You give it the tenant fixture,
the emergency access configuration,

107
00:06:48.014 --> 00:06:52.632
the drill record, a date to judge the
drills by, the thirtieth of October,

108
00:06:52.632 --> 00:06:54.861
an output folder, and, for the alert,

109
00:06:54.861 --> 00:06:58.842
the Log Analytics workspace,
the action group and a location.

110
00:06:58.842 --> 00:07:03.454
The check finds nine findings, no
warnings and one information item.

111
00:07:03.454 --> 00:07:08.093
It writes the alert rule to emergency-alert
dot json, for review, and tells

112
00:07:08.093 --> 00:07:13.117
you to deploy it with your reviewed
pipeline; it doesn't deploy anything itself.

113
00:07:13.117 --> 00:07:18.417
And as always, the manifest lists every
request and the hash of every file.

114
00:07:18.417 --> 00:07:22.187
On the slide, the two resource
I D's are wrapped to fit;

115
00:07:22.187 --> 00:07:25.770
on the command line, each
one is a single word.

116
00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:31.482
Step four prints the findings.

117
00:07:31.482 --> 00:07:34.558
E A oh one, the first account, passes.

118
00:07:34.558 --> 00:07:38.020
The second, E A oh two, fails five ways.

119
00:07:38.020 --> 00:07:42.483
It's on contoso dot example, not
the on-microsoft dot com domain.

120
00:07:42.483 --> 00:07:46.344
Its Global Administrator assignment
ends, so it isn't permanent.

121
00:07:46.344 --> 00:07:49.445
It has a phone method, which
isn't phishing-resistant.

122
00:07:49.445 --> 00:07:51.370
It isn't in the exclusion group.

123
00:07:51.370 --> 00:07:53.308
And its last drill is too old.

124
00:07:53.308 --> 00:07:54.595
Then the policies.

125
00:07:54.595 --> 00:07:59.770
Three enforced policies don't exclude the
accounts: the legacy authentication block,

126
00:07:59.770 --> 00:08:02.758
the compliant-device
policy for administrators,

127
00:08:02.758 --> 00:08:04.945
and the activation gate's own policy.

128
00:08:04.945 --> 00:08:07.745
Each of those could lock
the emergency accounts out.

129
00:08:07.745 --> 00:08:12.119
The report-only policy that
records emergency sign-ins is Info,

130
00:08:12.119 --> 00:08:13.970
as Microsoft Learn allows.

131
00:08:13.970 --> 00:08:18.064
And the last row, E A ten: carl
dot ops is in the exclusion group,

132
00:08:18.064 --> 00:08:23.870
and shouldn't be, because every policy
that excludes the group excludes him too.

133
00:08:27.620 --> 00:08:31.107
Step five matches the same
export with the example's rule.

134
00:08:31.107 --> 00:08:36.170
You load the export's rows and pass the
two emergency accounts' object I D's.

135
00:08:36.170 --> 00:08:40.020
Five rows come back, where
Microsoft Learn's query found two.

136
00:08:40.020 --> 00:08:43.545
The two interactive sign-ins
are there, one for each account.

137
00:08:43.545 --> 00:08:44.720
And three more.

138
00:08:44.720 --> 00:08:47.404
A non-interactive sign-in
by the first account,

139
00:08:47.404 --> 00:08:51.007
which is what a refreshed token
in a stolen session looks like.

140
00:08:51.007 --> 00:08:55.446
An Add member to role event made by
the second account: a role change.

141
00:08:55.446 --> 00:08:59.515
And a User registered security
info event on the first account:

142
00:08:59.515 --> 00:09:04.133
a new method registered on an emergency
account, the start of a takeover.

143
00:09:04.133 --> 00:09:08.480
Those are the three events the
extra two tables exist to catch.

144
00:09:12.230 --> 00:09:14.255
Step six shows the guards.

145
00:09:14.255 --> 00:09:18.443
Ask for the query with a user
principal name, E A oh one at the

146
00:09:18.443 --> 00:09:23.067
on-microsoft dot com domain, and it
refuses: that isn't an object I D.

147
00:09:23.067 --> 00:09:26.317
A query on names would miss
the account after a rename.

148
00:09:26.317 --> 00:09:28.817
Then read the alert
template the check wrote.

149
00:09:28.817 --> 00:09:33.206
Its resource is a Microsoft
Insights scheduled query rule,

150
00:09:33.206 --> 00:09:38.042
A P I version twenty twenty-three,
twelve, oh one, with severity zero.

151
00:09:38.042 --> 00:09:40.931
That's the reviewed template
your pipeline deploys.

152
00:09:40.931 --> 00:09:45.167
Step seven runs the whole test
suite: three hundred thirty pass.

153
00:09:45.167 --> 00:09:48.692
Then delete the output folder,
because it names the accounts.

154
00:09:48.692 --> 00:09:53.640
Pester prints its summary on several
lines; the slide shows it on one.

155
00:09:57.390 --> 00:10:01.209
Lab 2 runs in your own test
tenant, worldwide or G C C,

156
00:10:01.209 --> 00:10:05.028
with a test Azure
subscription, never production.

157
00:10:05.028 --> 00:10:07.921
You send the audit log
and the interactive and

158
00:10:07.921 --> 00:10:10.966
non-interactive sign-in
logs to a test workspace.

159
00:10:10.966 --> 00:10:14.662
You register a reader app, run
the check with your own accounts,

160
00:10:14.662 --> 00:10:17.127
and run the query by
hand in the workspace.

161
00:10:17.127 --> 00:10:21.766
Then you deploy the alert from the reviewed
template, and run a drill that fires it.

162
00:10:21.766 --> 00:10:26.810
If the test tenant's emergency accounts
are real, treat that drill as Microsoft

163
00:10:26.810 --> 00:10:31.390
Learn's validation drill: tell security
operations first, and record it.

164
00:10:31.390 --> 00:10:35.102
You need at least Security Administrator
for the diagnostic setting,

165
00:10:35.102 --> 00:10:37.407
the six read permissions
for the reader app,

166
00:10:37.407 --> 00:10:40.415
and at least Monitoring
Contributor to deploy the alert.

167
00:10:40.415 --> 00:10:45.235
The back-out deletes the alert rule and
action group, the permissions, the app and

168
00:10:45.235 --> 00:10:50.128
its certificate, the diagnostic setting if
the lab created it, and the test output.

169
00:10:50.128 --> 00:10:52.650
The authors haven't run Lab 2.

170
00:10:56.400 --> 00:10:57.787
The federal requirement.

171
00:10:57.787 --> 00:11:01.974
The check is the plan's Gate 1 test,
emergency access accounts tested,

172
00:11:01.974 --> 00:11:05.113
and the alert watches the
accounts between drills.

173
00:11:05.113 --> 00:11:09.616
It supports NIST special publication
eight hundred fifty-three A C two,

174
00:11:09.616 --> 00:11:13.433
with emergency accounts as a
defined account type, and I A two,

175
00:11:13.433 --> 00:11:17.937
enhancement one, multifactor
authentication to privileged accounts.

176
00:11:17.937 --> 00:11:23.837
The alert supports A C two,
enhancement four, A U six and S I four.

177
00:11:23.837 --> 00:11:28.982
And from CISA's B O D twenty-five
oh-one and its ScubaGear baseline:

178
00:11:28.982 --> 00:11:33.460
M S A A D seven point three,
cloud-only privileged accounts,

179
00:11:33.460 --> 00:11:39.176
and three point six, phishing-resistant
M F A for highly privileged roles.

180
00:11:39.176 --> 00:11:43.975
Treating the drills as compensating
controls is the workbook's design guidance.

181
00:11:43.975 --> 00:11:46.351
The evidence an ISSO keeps for Gate 1:

182
00:11:46.351 --> 00:11:49.914
the findings with no finding
from the test tenant's run;

183
00:11:49.914 --> 00:11:52.449
the drill tickets and the drill record;

184
00:11:52.449 --> 00:11:57.560
the reviewed alert template and its
deployment record; and the manifest.

185
00:12:01.310 --> 00:12:03.285
Check yourself with two questions.

186
00:12:03.285 --> 00:12:06.860
First: what is the lockout
Microsoft Learn documents?

187
00:12:06.860 --> 00:12:10.894
The answer is b: every Global
Administrator and Privileged Role

188
00:12:10.894 --> 00:12:15.385
Administrator eligible, approval
required, and no approvers selected.

189
00:12:15.385 --> 00:12:18.535
No one can approve, so
administration is locked.

190
00:12:18.535 --> 00:12:24.173
Second: a report-only Conditional Access
policy doesn't exclude the emergency group.

191
00:12:24.173 --> 00:12:25.923
What does E A oh eight report?

192
00:12:25.923 --> 00:12:29.142
The answer is b: Info,
because Microsoft Learn

193
00:12:29.142 --> 00:12:32.361
says report-only policies
need no exclusion.

194
00:12:32.361 --> 00:12:37.573
You saw it for the policy that records
emergency sign-ins in Lab 1, step four.

195
00:12:37.573 --> 00:12:42.840
The module page has two more questions,
each linked to the section that answers it.

196
00:12:46.590 --> 00:12:47.415
To sum up.

197
00:12:47.415 --> 00:12:52.165
You checked two emergency accounts against
Microsoft Learn's standard and found one

198
00:12:52.165 --> 00:12:56.045
that fails five ways, three enforced
policies that would lock them out,

199
00:12:56.045 --> 00:12:58.253
and a stranger in their exclusion group.

200
00:12:58.253 --> 00:13:02.578
And you built an alert that sees five
events where Learn's sample sees two.

201
00:13:02.578 --> 00:13:06.745
Carry one thing forward: before
any later lab enforces anything,

202
00:13:06.745 --> 00:13:10.440
build the emergency accounts
and confirm one can sign in.

203
00:13:10.440 --> 00:13:16.215
Next is Module 3: Conditional Access for
administrators and the activation gate.

204
00:13:16.215 --> 00:13:21.034
It's the first module that writes, so it's
the first that could lock people out,

205
00:13:21.034 --> 00:13:22.744
and its first rule, C A oh one,

206
00:13:22.744 --> 00:13:26.620
keeps the emergency group
excluded from every policy file.
